<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/timber-frame.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/timber-frame-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/timber-frame.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">›</span>Appraisal process</nav><span class="kicker on-dark">Process guide</span><h1>The Vermont Insurance Appraisal Process, Step by Step</h1><p class="lede">Eight stages from the first disagreement to a signed award: what happens, who does what and where files most often go wrong.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn light" href="/claim-review-request/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts"><div><small>Stages</small><b>8</b></div><div><small>Panel</small><b>Two appraisers + umpire</b></div><div><small>Binding</small><b>Two of three signatures</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>The appraisal condition in most Vermont property policies follows a familiar pattern, but the details vary. Use this guide to understand the steps, then read your own policy’s wording, which controls.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Before you start.</b> Appraisal decides the amount of loss. It is not designed to settle coverage disputes, claims of delay or bad faith. Vermont’s insurance trade practices law (Title 8) lists unfair claim settlement practices, and Vermont courts have recognized bad-faith claims against insurers in some circumstances. Those are questions for the Department of Financial Regulation or a Vermont attorney.</div>
<h2 id="step-1">Step 1: Pin down the disagreement</h2><p>Appraisal is for one kind of dispute: the carrier accepts that the loss is covered but you disagree on how much it costs. Get the carrier’s full estimate and any letters explaining its position. Compare it with your contractor’s estimate so you know exactly where the numbers differ.</p><h2 id="step-2">Step 2: Read the appraisal condition</h2><p>Find the Appraisal paragraph in the conditions section of your policy. Note who can demand appraisal, how the demand must be made, the time to name an appraiser (often 20 days) and how the umpire is chosen. Look for any endorsement that changes the standard wording.</p><h2 id="step-3">Step 3: Send a written demand</h2><p>Either side can usually invoke appraisal in writing. A clear demand identifies the claim, states that you disagree on the amount of loss, invokes the appraisal provision and names your appraiser or says you will within the policy deadline. Keep proof of delivery.</p><h2 id="step-4">Step 4: Each side names an appraiser</h2><p>You choose a competent and impartial appraiser, and so does the carrier. Each side pays its own. Your appraiser inspects the property, builds an independent estimate and gathers the photos and measurements to support it.</p><h2 id="step-5">Step 5: The appraisers select an umpire</h2><p>The two appraisers try to agree on a neutral umpire, often within 15 days. If they cannot, either side may usually ask a court in the county where the property is located to appoint one. The umpire’s fee is normally split.</p><h2 id="step-6">Step 6: The appraisers work the differences</h2><p>The appraisers compare estimates line by line. Most items are resolved here, sometimes on site. Anything they agree on is set; only disputed items move on.</p><h2 id="step-7">Step 7: The umpire decides what is left</h2><p>The umpire reviews the remaining items, sometimes with an inspection and written submissions from each appraiser, then joins one appraiser or the other on each item.</p><h2 id="step-8">Step 8: The award is signed and paid</h2><p>An award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss. The carrier then pays according to the policy: the award, less the deductible, prior payments and any depreciation not yet recoverable.</p>
<h2>How long it takes</h2>
<p>A straightforward residential appraisal often resolves in one to three months after the demand. Larger or more technical claims, winter access problems and umpire scheduling can stretch that. Preparing your file before the demand is the best way to keep it moving.</p>
<h2>What it costs</h2>
<p>Each side pays its own appraiser and usually half of the umpire. Fee arrangements include flat fees, hourly rates and, sometimes, a percentage of the increase. Our <a href="/vermont-claim-tools/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap &amp; fee estimator</a> shows how fees affect your net result. Example figures on this site are illustrative.</p>
<h2>Where claims go wrong</h2>
<ul>
<li>Demanding appraisal before getting a complete, itemized estimate of your own.</li>
<li>Letting the deadline to name an appraiser pass.</li>
<li>Mixing excluded flood damage into a homeowners appraisal.</li>
<li>Choosing an appraiser who cannot meet the policy’s impartiality standard.</li>
<li>Forgetting that the suit-limitation clock may keep running during appraisal.</li>
</ul>
</article>
